from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-3731';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Theorem 1.5, constant rank and inseparability promises for restricted polynomial representations; Section 6 second question, explicitly contrasting a criterion with AM∩coAM.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='Constructing Faithful Homomorphisms over Fields of Finite Characteristic',authors='Prerona Chatterjee; Ramprasad Saptharishi',year=2022,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.10249v3',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1812.10249v3',locator='Revision submitted 13 December 2022; PDF dated 14 December. Definitions 2.4–2.6 and Remark 2.5 give the nonstandard inseparability parameter; Section 6 retains the qualitative question.'),dict(id='criterion',title='Algebraic Independence over Positive Characteristic: New Criterion and Applications to Locally Low-Algebraic-Rank Circuits',authors='Anurag Pandey; Nitin Saxena; Amit Sinhababu',year=2018,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/s00037-018-0167-5',pdf_url='https://www.cse.iitk.ac.in/users/nitin/papers/indep-low-insep-full.pdf',locator='Computational Complexity 27, 617–670; Theorem 1.1, randomized poly(s, binomial(n+t,n)) independence test under an inseparability bound; field-theory definitions.'),dict(id='interactive',title='Algebraic Dependencies and PSPACE Algorithms in Approximative Complexity',authors='Zeyu Guo; Nitin Saxena; Amit Sinhababu',year=2018,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CCC.2018.10',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.09275',locator='CCC 2018; finite-field algebraic-dependence/independence decision in AM∩coAM; the source already cites this as avoiding an inseparability promise.')]
issue='The source asks how essential inseparability is, but supplies no single proposition specifying the desired improvement. Its criterion-based randomized independence test, deterministic faithful-map construction for restricted inputs, and already-known AM∩coAM decision protocols are different targets. A completed card must choose which theorem or computational task is to improve; specify input representation, finite-field encoding, resource and error bound; and state the allowed dependence on degree, rank and inseparability. The source and its December 2022 revision do not select these choices. Replacing the question by unrestricted polynomial-time independence testing would be a new, stronger target, not a recovered equivalence.'
notes=['Recovered the nonstandard inseparability parameter used by the source: the least common Frobenius power reaching the separable part, not the usual extension degree.','Defined algebraic independence, rank, the coordinate-completion minimum and faithful maps.','Identified the explicit randomized criterion bound and distinguished it from the restricted deterministic construction and interactive-protocol result.','Checked the December 2022 revision and later references; the original question remains qualitative, so no new polynomial-time conjecture is certified.']
save(i,dict(formal='The source asks whether dependence on inseparable degree is essential in testing algebraic independence over positive-characteristic fields. It contrasts a randomized criterion-based test whose cost depends on that parameter with already-known AM∩coAM decision protocols that have no such promise. This is a research direction, not yet one quantified algorithmic proposition: the source does not say whether the desired advance is a faster ordinary test, a new criterion, or removal of the promise from its restricted faithful-homomorphism construction. The exact target and resource bound remain to be specified.',definitions='For a field F of characteristic p>0 and polynomials f₁,…,f_m∈F[x₁,…,x_n], algebraic independence over F means that no nonzero A∈F[z₁,…,z_m] satisfies A(f₁,…,f_m)=0. Their algebraic rank k is the maximum size of an independent subfamily; equivalently k=trdeg_F F(f₁,…,f_m), where F(f) denotes the field generated by these polynomials and F. An algebraic element is separable if its irreducible minimal polynomial has no repeated root. For a finite algebraic extension K/L in characteristic p, write Sep(K/L) for the elements of K separable over L. The source’s parameter t(K/L) is the least p-power p^e, e≥0, such that every a∈K has a^{p^e}∈Sep(K/L). It is one for a separable extension. This is generally different from the conventional inseparable degree [K:Sep(K/L)]; for example F_p(x,y)/F_p(x^p,y^p) has source parameter p and conventional degree p². For a polynomial family f of rank k, consider coordinate sets S⊆[n] of size n−k such that x_S is algebraically independent over F(f). Then F(x)/F(f,x_S) is finite algebraic. Define t(f) as the minimum of t(F(x)/F(f,x_S)) over such S. This is the coordinate-completion convention in full-version Definition 2.6; such sets exist by extending a transcendence basis with coordinates. An F-algebra homomorphism Φ on F[x] is faithful for f if trdeg_F F(Φ(f₁),…,Φ(f_m))=k. Preserving rank over F and preserving it over a larger coefficient field are distinct statements. The source’s deterministic construction concerns sparse polynomials and certain products of variable-disjoint multilinear sparse polynomials under constant k and t. A sparse representation lists nonzero monomials and coefficients; a circuit representation is an arithmetic directed acyclic graph and may succinctly represent exponentially large degree. The broader randomized independence theorem concerns circuit inputs over finite fields and has time polynomial in s and binomial(n+t,n), where s is the input size and t is a promised bound in the independent case. AM denotes languages with polynomial-size, constant-round public-coin interactive proofs, with completeness at least 2/3 and soundness at most 1/3; coAM comprises complements of AM languages. Membership in both classes gives separate protocols for the two answers, not a randomized polynomial-time decision algorithm without a prover. A particular finite-field encoding, resource model and desired bound are not selected for the open direction.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Recover a single source-supported claim with explicit input representation and parameters, a specified algorithmic or algebraic output, allowed randomness or interaction, and an exact resource or structural improvement. Then prove or refute that claim. Restating the existing AM∩coAM result, or removing the parameter from an unrelated theorem, does not answer an unspecified new improvement. This card is not certified as a completed open benchmark until that target is fixed.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The parameter measures failure of ordinary derivatives',text='Independent p-th powers can have zero formal derivatives. The source’s higher-order criterion therefore uses an inseparability parameter, and its exact definition matters: it is a Frobenius-power bound rather than the usual field-extension index.',citation='full'),dict(title='The existing ordinary test has an explicit dependence',text='The cited theorem gives a randomized independence test in time polynomial in the input size and binomial(n+t,n). A constant t makes that dependence polynomial in n, while general circuit inputs need not have constant t.',citation='criterion'),dict(title='Interactive verification already avoids that promise',text='The finite-field decision problem lies in AM∩coAM without bounding t. The original question explicitly mentions this result, so the mere existence of any parameter-free characterization cannot be the missing advance.',citation='interactive'),dict(title='Faithful maps impose another output requirement',text='The source constructs polynomial-size lists of candidate homomorphisms for specified sparse circuit forms when rank and t are constant. This constructive statement is stronger in a different direction than deciding independence and is not supplied by an interactive protocol.',citation='primary')],why='The direction concerns a basic obstruction to extending algebraic-independence methods to small characteristic, but its scientific content depends on separating ordinary algorithms, interactive verification and explicit rank-preserving constructions.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2018',text='The criterion gives an inseparability-dependent randomized bound; separate work places finite-field independence testing in AM∩coAM without the same promise.',citation='criterion'),dict(date='2019',text='Restricted faithful homomorphisms are constructed with constant rank and inseparability; the concluding question asks how essential the parameter is.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2022-12-13',text='The revised full source retains the qualitative question and explicitly defines its nonstandard inseparability parameter.',citation='full')],status='uncertain',status_note='Read the field-theory definitions in the December 2022 full revision, the criterion theorem and the finite-field interactive result. A May 2025 TIFR seminar still describes the ordinary finite-field testing problem as open. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no source that turns this qualitative direction into the specific benchmark target requested by the card. No general new algorithmic conjecture is invented.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(status='needs_specification',reviewed_on=DATE,notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue),review_note=issue,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A structural obstacle in positive-characteristic algebraic complexity and identity testing, with several different computational formulations that must not be conflated. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Algebraic independence means that no nonzero polynomial relation holds among the input polynomials.','In small characteristic, the source uses a Frobenius-power inseparability parameter in its criterion and restricted faithful-map construction.','That parameter is not the conventional inseparable extension degree.','Interactive AM∩coAM verification already avoids the parameter promise, but does not give an ordinary randomized polynomial-time algorithm.','The source does not select which stronger test or construction should lose its dependence, so an exact new target remains to be specified.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','criterion','interactive'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://www.tcs.tifr.res.in/web/events/1553'],state='improved_unfinished')
